tional self-efficacy beliefs influence functional capacity evaluation</article-title><source>J Occup Rehabil</source><year>2007</year><volume>17</volume><fpage>73</fpage><lpage>82</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1007/s10926-007-9068-1</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Asante AK, Brintnell ES, Gross DP. Functional self-efficacy beliefs influence functional capacity evaluation. J Occup Rehabil. 2007;17:73&#x02013;82. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17235677</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR9"><label>9.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rudy</surname><given-names>TE</given-names></name><name><surname>Lieber</surname><given-names>SJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Boston</surname><given-names>JR</given-names></name><name><surname>Gourley</surname><given-names>LM</given-names></name><name><surname>Baysal</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Psychosocial predictors of physical performance in disabled individuals with chronic pain</article-title><source>Clin J Pain</source><year>2003</year><volume>19</volume><fpage>18</fpage><lpage>30</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1097/00002508-200301000-00003</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Rudy TE, Lieber SJ, Boston JR, Gourley LM, Baysal E. Psychosocial predictors of physical performance in disabled individuals with chronic pain. Clin J Pain. 2003;19:18&#x02013;30. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12514453</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR10"><label>10.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Filho</surname><given-names>IT</given-names></name><name><surname>Simmonds</surname><given-names>MJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Protas</surname><given-names>EJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Jones</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Back pain, physical function, and estimat